Setup. Consider an early-universe cosmological scenario in which a pseudo-scalar field φ (the QCD axion), with shift-symmetric coupling

  L ⊃ (φ/f) · (g_s^2 / 32π^2) · G^{aμν} \tilde G^a_{μν}

to the Standard Model SU(3)_c gluon field strength G (with dual \tilde G) and decay constant f, plays the role of the inflaton driving a phase of accelerated expansion. "Warm inflation" means a regime in which a thermal bath at temperature T ≳ H (with H the Hubble rate) is continuously sourced during inflation by a dissipative friction Γ φ̇ in the φ equation of motion,

  φ̈ + (3H + Γ) φ̇ + V'(φ) = 0,

with Γ arising from QCD sphaleron heating. It is known that in pure Yang–Mills the sphaleron rate produces a friction coefficient Γ ∝ α_s^5 T^3/f^2 sufficient for sustained warm inflation, but the presence of light fermions charged under SU(3)_c (the up, down, and strange quarks of the Standard Model with their measured masses m_q ≪ T_inf) generates an axial chemical potential that drives the sphaleron-induced friction to zero, obstructing warm inflation.

Problem. Construct a fully viable cosmological model, consistent with all current constraints (slow-roll, perturbation amplitude and tilt observed in the CMB, tensor-to-scalar ratio bounds, successful reheating into the Standard Model, post-inflationary recovery of the measured light-quark masses, and the standard solution of the strong-CP problem), in which:

(i) the QCD axion φ itself is the inflaton;
(ii) the radiation bath during inflation consists of QCD degrees of freedom sourced through the φ G \tilde G coupling;
(iii) the chemical-potential obstruction from light Standard Model quarks is evaded — for example by an early-universe mechanism that makes the up, down, and strange quark masses sufficiently large (m_q ≫ T_inf) during inflation while relaxing to their present values afterwards.

Either exhibit such a model explicitly with calculable parameter ranges, or prove that no such model can satisfy all of the above requirements simultaneously.

